WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.536
-My next guest is the host of
"WTF with Marc Maron."

00:00:04.536 --> 00:00:08.308
And on May 16th, he'll be
honored by the Podcast Academy

00:00:08.308 --> 00:00:11.177
as the first ever recipient
of the Governor's Award.

00:00:11.177 --> 00:00:13.312
Here is Marc Maron!
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:13.312 --> 00:00:14.847
Look at that.

00:00:14.847 --> 00:00:17.250
That's what I'm talking about!
Yes!

00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:18.851
-What's up?

00:00:18.851 --> 00:00:21.353
-It's good to see you, bud.
I have to ask you a question

00:00:21.353 --> 00:00:23.689
'cause I know that
you got a chance to finally

00:00:23.689 --> 00:00:25.792
visit your mom
recently in Florida.

00:00:25.792 --> 00:00:28.661
-I did. A while -- you know,
after I got the vaccine,

00:00:28.661 --> 00:00:31.297
and right after, I went through
the two weeks,

00:00:31.297 --> 00:00:33.633
I went right to Florida
to see my mother.

00:00:33.633 --> 00:00:35.668
I hadn't seen her
in a year and a half.

00:00:35.668 --> 00:00:38.438
It was her birthday recently.
She just turned 60, again.

00:00:38.438 --> 00:00:41.841
[ Laughter ]

00:00:41.841 --> 00:00:45.411
She turns 60 every -- Every year
now for 20 years, she's 60.

00:00:45.411 --> 00:00:48.581
And we just let it slide.
[ Laughter ]

00:00:48.581 --> 00:00:50.349
But you know what's amazing,
Jimmy?

00:00:50.349 --> 00:00:52.751
No matter how long you don't see
your mother for,

00:00:52.751 --> 00:00:55.555
as soon as you get there,
within 10 minutes,

00:00:55.555 --> 00:00:58.757
you're an angry high schooler.
[ Laughter ]

00:00:58.757 --> 00:01:02.028
Within 10 minutes, I was like,
"What?! What do you want?! No!"

00:01:02.028 --> 00:01:03.763
[ Laughter ]

00:01:03.763 --> 00:01:05.732
So here's what you do, Jim.
-Okay.

00:01:05.732 --> 00:01:07.333
-Whenever you go,
when you're grown up,

00:01:07.333 --> 00:01:09.535
if you can afford it,
never stay at their house.

00:01:09.535 --> 00:01:11.938
Never stay
at your mother's house.

00:01:11.938 --> 00:01:14.140
Always stay at a hotel nearby.

00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:16.810
Not too nearby, so it gives you
time to process.

00:01:16.810 --> 00:01:17.977
'Cause then you make plans.

00:01:17.977 --> 00:01:19.812
They're like, "Do you want to go
to lunch?"

00:01:19.812 --> 00:01:21.081
And you go, "Yes."

00:01:21.081 --> 00:01:22.449
And they'll go, "Okay.
When you coming?"

00:01:22.449 --> 00:01:24.617
Like, "I'll be over
about an hour."

00:01:24.617 --> 00:01:27.086
Then for an hour,
you can try to reel it in,

00:01:27.086 --> 00:01:28.454
pull yourself together.

00:01:28.454 --> 00:01:31.224
So when you get there, you don't
yell or cry or storm away.

00:01:31.224 --> 00:01:32.992
[ Laughter ]

00:01:32.992 --> 00:01:34.526
-You never stay with your mom?

00:01:34.526 --> 00:01:37.830
-No. No. No. That's horrifying.

00:01:37.830 --> 00:01:40.099
Could you imagine waking up
and they're just there?

00:01:40.099 --> 00:01:41.200
[ Laughter ]

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:43.570
I'm a grown man.
I can't do that.

00:01:43.570 --> 00:01:46.672
-Is this the first time
traveling for you?

00:01:46.672 --> 00:01:48.574
-Yes. I'm not scared anymore.

00:01:48.574 --> 00:01:51.010
I got the vaccine.
I'm just going full steam ahead.

00:01:51.010 --> 00:01:53.346
And Florida was a good choice,

00:01:53.346 --> 00:01:56.349
because, you know, I hadn't
really been around people.

00:01:56.349 --> 00:01:57.950
And when you go to Florida,

00:01:57.950 --> 00:02:01.087
you're around the full spectrum
of hideous humanity.

00:02:01.087 --> 00:02:03.388
[ Laughter ]

00:02:03.388 --> 00:02:05.457
And they're half dressed.

00:02:05.457 --> 00:02:07.894
Now, I'm not saying
that I'm not part of that.

00:02:07.894 --> 00:02:09.362
I'm that judging.
We're all hideous.

00:02:09.362 --> 00:02:11.664
But I'm about midway,
about middle hideous.

00:02:11.664 --> 00:02:14.066
In Florida, you know what
I learned, Jimmy,

00:02:14.066 --> 00:02:16.603
is that the expression
"does not disappoint"

00:02:16.603 --> 00:02:19.439
goes for both good things
and horrible things.

00:02:19.439 --> 00:02:21.707
So, here's what you see
in Florida.

00:02:21.707 --> 00:02:25.944
It's like there's a whole
population of elderly people

00:02:25.944 --> 00:02:28.447
that just give zero [bleep] --
Can I say that?

00:02:28.447 --> 00:02:30.949
[ Laughter ]
They give zero.

00:02:30.949 --> 00:02:32.851
Okay? And it's beautiful.

00:02:32.851 --> 00:02:35.021
I saw a man wearing --

00:02:35.021 --> 00:02:37.356
I think it must have been
a Speedo unitard.

00:02:37.356 --> 00:02:40.593
[ Laughter ]
Okay, like, a Speedo onesie.

00:02:40.593 --> 00:02:43.696
And he was on a unicycle.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:43.696 --> 00:02:47.800
And, Jim, he was 92
if he was a day.

00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:48.901
92!
-Wow!

00:02:48.901 --> 00:02:50.870
-Everything packed
into the onesie.

00:02:50.870 --> 00:02:54.440
And on some level, I think,
"God bless that guy."

00:02:54.440 --> 00:02:57.544
You know, if that's who --
I think all senior citizens,

00:02:57.544 --> 00:03:01.114
if you can't do that in reality,
at least do it in your heart.

00:03:01.114 --> 00:03:04.717
Be the man on the unicycle
in the unitard.

00:03:04.717 --> 00:03:07.052
-Yes, I totally agree with you.
Yeah.

00:03:07.052 --> 00:03:08.854
Did you go out to dinner at all

00:03:08.854 --> 00:03:10.256
while you were there
with your mom?

00:03:10.256 --> 00:03:14.126
-Oh, man.
Look, I'm not a complain-y guy.

00:03:14.126 --> 00:03:16.061
Alright, we went out to a place,
alright --

00:03:16.061 --> 00:03:17.230
we went out to a place

00:03:17.230 --> 00:03:19.431
where it was a seafood place
on the beach,

00:03:19.431 --> 00:03:22.135
which, if you need to know,
if there is a seafood place

00:03:22.135 --> 00:03:24.136
on the beach in a tourist town
in Florida,

00:03:24.136 --> 00:03:25.705
they don't care
if you ever come back.

00:03:25.705 --> 00:03:28.407
That's what I learned.
So, we got this food.

00:03:28.407 --> 00:03:30.643
It wasn't good.
Now, here's what happens.

00:03:30.643 --> 00:03:32.311
The next morning, because I made

00:03:32.311 --> 00:03:34.113
the reservation online,
they send me the little,

00:03:34.113 --> 00:03:36.115
"How was your experience
at blah blah blah?"

00:03:36.115 --> 00:03:38.318
So I'm like, "I'm going to tell
them. I'm going to tell them."

00:03:38.318 --> 00:03:39.518
And while I'm writing this,

00:03:39.518 --> 00:03:41.353
I see that something happens
on my phone.

00:03:41.353 --> 00:03:42.955
It picks up a voicemail.

00:03:42.955 --> 00:03:44.123
But I write this thing,
and I'm like,

00:03:44.123 --> 00:03:46.559
"The clams were disgusting.
They were stinky.

00:03:46.559 --> 00:03:49.261
The lobster on
the chilled platter was warm.

00:03:49.261 --> 00:03:52.965
The crab was horrible.
And the oysters were just okay."

00:03:52.965 --> 00:03:54.600
-Do you do this ever?
Are you a Yelp reviewer?

00:03:54.600 --> 00:03:56.201
-Never. I never do it.

00:03:56.201 --> 00:03:58.971
And at the end of it, I said, "I
know you probably know all this,

00:03:58.971 --> 00:04:00.707
but you really don't care,
do you?

00:04:00.707 --> 00:04:01.974
Because you're on the beach

00:04:01.974 --> 00:04:03.576
and you don't care
if people come back."

00:04:03.576 --> 00:04:05.011
And I send that off, alright?

00:04:05.011 --> 00:04:06.579
-Wow!

00:04:06.579 --> 00:04:09.215
-So then I check my phone,
and it's a message

00:04:09.215 --> 00:04:11.984
just by coincidence from the
manager -- the general manager

00:04:11.984 --> 00:04:14.953
of that restaurant, saying
I left my credit card there.

00:04:14.953 --> 00:04:16.389
[ Laughter ]
Now I'm like, "Oh, my God."

00:04:16.389 --> 00:04:19.592
Alright, so now I got to --

00:04:19.592 --> 00:04:21.761
I got to call the guy, right?

00:04:21.761 --> 00:04:24.396
So I'm pretty sure in my head

00:04:24.396 --> 00:04:26.866
that they don't read
these things.

00:04:26.866 --> 00:04:28.501
Whatever. So I call the guy.

00:04:28.501 --> 00:04:31.637
And I'm like,
"Hey, yeah, it's Marc Maron.

00:04:31.637 --> 00:04:32.938
You called about
my credit card?"

00:04:32.938 --> 00:04:35.307
He's like, "Yeah, I just --
just got the e-mail."

00:04:35.307 --> 00:04:38.277
I'm like, "Oh, man. Yeah."

00:04:38.277 --> 00:04:40.947
He's like, "It was like
a kick in the gut."

00:04:40.947 --> 00:04:43.516
And, like, there's that moment,
Jim, where you're like --

00:04:43.516 --> 00:04:47.487
The Internet is for anonymous,
one-sided attacks

00:04:47.487 --> 00:04:49.756
that you don't have to
answer for in real life.

00:04:49.756 --> 00:04:51.957
-Yeah, of course. Of course.

00:04:51.957 --> 00:04:53.626
-So now I got to answer for it,
right?

00:04:53.626 --> 00:04:57.697
So now, I tell the guy -- I tell
the guy, "Look, man, I hope --

00:04:57.697 --> 00:04:59.832
I didn't mean to hurt your
feelings, but it was terrible."

00:04:59.832 --> 00:05:02.201
He's like, "I don't get it, man.
I run a good restaurant.

00:05:02.201 --> 00:05:04.169
We get the fresh fish every day.
The clams --"

00:05:04.169 --> 00:05:06.906
I'm like, "The clams were
not good. The clams --"

00:05:06.906 --> 00:05:08.374
He's like,
"Well, they opened them fresh."

00:05:08.374 --> 00:05:10.642
I'm like, "Yeah, but, you know,
just 'cause a clam is closed

00:05:10.642 --> 00:05:12.845
doesn't mean it's good.
You know what I mean?"

00:05:12.845 --> 00:05:14.914
So I do all that.
-My God.

00:05:14.914 --> 00:05:19.118
-And then he goes --
Then he says to me, he says,

00:05:19.118 --> 00:05:21.020
"You know, there's a comedian
with your name, Marc Maron."

00:05:21.020 --> 00:05:22.321
I'm like, "Oh.

00:05:22.321 --> 00:05:23.956
Um, yeah, I'm him."

00:05:23.956 --> 00:05:26.125
He's like, "Oh, really?
Well, my kids love you."

00:05:26.125 --> 00:05:28.761
And I'm like, "I don't care.
The clams were bad, dude.

00:05:28.761 --> 00:05:31.197
The clams were bad."
[ Laughter ]

00:05:31.197 --> 00:05:33.199
"The lobster -- You're not gonna
make me feel bad.

00:05:33.199 --> 00:05:34.367
Your restaurant's terrible.

00:05:34.367 --> 00:05:36.301
But look, I'll come down,
get my credit card,

00:05:36.301 --> 00:05:38.904
we'll take a picture for your
kids, and it'll be fine."

00:05:38.904 --> 00:05:40.773
He's like, "I'm just sad
that your experience

00:05:40.773 --> 00:05:42.174
wasn't as good
as it could have been."

00:05:42.174 --> 00:05:44.543
I get down there, gives me
the card, we take the picture.

00:05:44.543 --> 00:05:46.912
I'm walking off. He's still
looking at me like, "Huh?"

00:05:46.912 --> 00:05:50.416
And I'm like, "Nope, nope, check
the clams. It was not good."

00:05:50.416 --> 00:05:52.985
-Good for you. Good for you.
-Yeah.

00:05:52.985 --> 00:05:56.088
I was willing to die on that
mountain, the clam mountain.

00:05:56.088 --> 00:05:59.191
-Good. I like that,
the clam mountain.

00:05:59.191 --> 00:06:03.362
Marc, next weekend you're
getting the Governor's Award

00:06:03.362 --> 00:06:06.365
for the --
from the Podcast Academy.

00:06:06.365 --> 00:06:10.002
This is the first time
they're ever doing this award.

00:06:10.002 --> 00:06:11.470
And it's a big deal.

00:06:11.470 --> 00:06:15.141
And I don't know. Does
Marc Maron care about awards?

00:06:15.141 --> 00:06:17.442
-Well, you know, it's weird.
I never win them.

00:06:17.442 --> 00:06:20.245
I've never won anything,
not since I came in second

00:06:20.245 --> 00:06:23.982
in the San Francisco Comedy
Competition I think in 1994.

00:06:23.982 --> 00:06:27.220
So I never win them,
and it's just ironic to me

00:06:27.220 --> 00:06:29.988
that they had to create a new
award for me to finally win one.

00:06:29.988 --> 00:06:33.058
[ Laughter ]
Now, the podcast award --

00:06:33.058 --> 00:06:35.094
The Podcast Academy
is legitimate.

00:06:35.094 --> 00:06:36.962
I don't want to disregard it.

00:06:36.962 --> 00:06:38.398
There is a governing body.

00:06:38.398 --> 00:06:39.999
They're legitimate people.
It's nice.

00:06:39.999 --> 00:06:41.166
It's an important award.

00:06:41.166 --> 00:06:42.435
It's the Governor's Award,

00:06:42.435 --> 00:06:43.502
which basically means like,

00:06:43.502 --> 00:06:44.970
"These guys have
doing it awhile.

00:06:44.970 --> 00:06:46.639
We should throw them a bone."

00:06:46.639 --> 00:06:48.741
So that's what the award is.

00:06:48.741 --> 00:06:51.777
It's for being a pioneer,
one of the originals,

00:06:51.777 --> 00:06:54.814
one of the people that sort of
defined the medium, a legend.

00:06:54.814 --> 00:06:57.383
And it's usually one of those
things -- ones they give you

00:06:57.383 --> 00:06:59.485
after people start caring about
who you are.

00:06:59.485 --> 00:07:01.687
But I'm going to take it, Jimmy.
[ Laughter ]

00:07:01.687 --> 00:07:03.155
-I think you deserve it.
You were there.

00:07:03.155 --> 00:07:04.757
You started it when
there was no one doing this.

00:07:04.757 --> 00:07:06.325
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:06.325 --> 00:07:08.160
-No, it's very exciting.

00:07:08.160 --> 00:07:11.764
I'm very -- It's an award
that I feel like I earned,

00:07:11.764 --> 00:07:13.165
and I'm proud of it.

00:07:13.165 --> 00:07:16.702
And me and my producing partner,
Brendan, we're excited about it.

00:07:16.702 --> 00:07:18.204
-You're one of the best
out there, buddy.

00:07:18.204 --> 00:07:19.805
And I always love
having you as a guest.

00:07:19.805 --> 00:07:21.641
Congratulations, early,
on this award.

00:07:21.641 --> 00:07:23.776
You deserve it, pal.
Marc Maron, everybody.

00:07:23.776 --> 00:07:27.880
Check out his award-winning
podcast, "WTF with Marc Maron."

